Z||000003||Voytek||11-13-2001||06:11 PM||||What year is your Fiero?  If it's pre-'88 then it's a bit easier.  Then you can attach the piston/rod part of the strut right into the louver bracket (there are two bolts on the side of the bracket - just remove one of them and install the strut).<P>I got mine off an '80's Cadillac (had 1 strut on each side of the hood).  Virtually brand new for about $5 bucks.  Other cars (like Skylarks) will fit too.<P>With the Caddy one, the hole on the rod side is actually a bit small so you can drill it out just enough and make a PERFECT fit.<P>I have the Caddy strut with a Skylark bolt (half threaded/half oversized and smooth) on the bracket/rod side so that the bolt threads right up to the smooth part and still leaves enough space for the strut to operate easily.<P>The whole procedure took about 1/2 an hour.<P>There are a couple of different lengths, depending on the car.  They are within about an inch or so and don't make much of a difference.<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||11-13-2001||08:48 PM||||By the time I drive to the boneyard and spend bucks for gas and few dollars to boneyard, I had rather buy Rodney's, at least I know it will fit and work.||||reg||1
